-- title:
--   Heights of 2× 2 minors of two tuples
-- statement:
--   Let $r$ be a natural number. The assertion is that there is a real constant $C_0$, depending only on $r$, with the following property: for all families $x, y : \mathrm{Fin}\ r \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of algebraic numbers (the algebraic closure of $\mathbb{Q}$ being the fixed model `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`), the quantity `absLogHeight` of the family indexed by $\mathrm{Fin}\ r \times \mathrm{Fin}\ r$ whose value at $(i,j)$ is the minor $x_i y_j - x_j y_i$ is bounded by `absLogHeight` $x$ + `absLogHeight` $y$ + $C_0$. Here, for a finite index type $\iota$ and a family $z : \iota \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, `absLogHeight z` is defined as the product of $(\mathrm{finrank}_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}(z_i : i \in \iota))^{-1}$ with `Height.logHeight` of $z$ regarded as a family of elements of the intermediate field $\mathbb{Q}(z_i : i \in \iota)$ generated by the range of $z$ (a finite extension of $\mathbb{Q}$); that is, the logarithmic height of the tuple over that number field, normalised by its degree. Note that the index set of minors runs over all ordered pairs, so the diagonal entries $0$ and both signs $\pm(x_i y_j - x_j y_i)$ occur. No positivity or nonvanishing hypothesis is imposed on $x$, $y$, and $r = 0$ is allowed.
--
--   This is the standard comparison of the absolute logarithmic height of an exterior square (Plücker-type coordinates) with the heights of the two factors, in the normalised form that is independent of the field of definition. It is used in the height-theoretic estimates for the degree-zero divisor class group of the modular curve, being cited by [`AlgebraicCurve.exists_forall_neg_le_pairHt`](thm.html#AlgebraicCurve.exists_forall_neg_le_pairHt).
